WEBVTT

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:07.940
<v Ira Glass>Jordan did not love the idea of coming into the studio to tell this next story. The very first thing he said to me when he sat down was--
<v Ira Glass>对于参与录制下一段故事，Jordan并不是很感兴趣。他坐下后跟我说的第一句话是——

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:17.360
<v Jordan Riley>I am curious-- of all the stories, why this story? Because it's not my particularly proudest story.
<v Jordan Riley>我很好奇——在所有的故事里，为什么要选这个？因为这并不是我最引以为傲的故事。

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:36.460
<v Ira Glass>In fact, the only reason that he agreed to the interview is that he's good friends with the little brother of one of our producers, Miki. They all grew up together. And this story happened when he was going into his senior year of high school in small town Utah. Jordan and his buddies all lived together in a house that summer, daring each other into Jackass style pranks and stunts.
<v Ira Glass>事实上，他之所以同意接受采访，完全是因为他与我们制片人Miki的弟弟是好朋友。他们是一起长大的。这个故事发生在高中最后一年，地点在犹他州的一个小镇。那个夏天，Jordan和他的哥们儿住在一起，他们总是互相怂恿，玩一些类似《蠢蛋搞怪秀》(Jackass)风格的恶作剧和特技。

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:48.280
<v Ira Glass>For instance, there's this time they drove a car into an orchard. There's the bike accident that gave Jordan one huge swollen testicle. There's the time they peed into a 7 Up can, and Miki's mom accidentally drank out of it.
<v Ira Glass>比如，有一次他们把车开进了果园。还有一次自行车事故，导致Jordan的一个睾丸肿得很大。还有一次，他们往7 Up汽水罐里撒尿，Miki的妈妈不小心喝了下去。

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.620
<v Jordan Riley>I'll tell you it was not my pee in the 7 UP can.
<v Jordan Riley>我得告诉你，那罐7 Up里的尿不是我的。

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:52.230
<v Ira Glass>All right.
<v Ira Glass>好吧。

00:00:52.230 --> 00:01:07.790
<v Jordan Riley>And I feel like to talk on public radio, there's a part of me that would rather tell America at large about my swollen testicles story. But this is the one that I feel I tell the least.
<v Jordan Riley>我觉得在公共广播电台讲话，我宁愿告诉全美国关于我睾丸肿胀的故事。但这个故事是我觉得最少提及的。

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:38.280
<v Ira Glass>The story that he tells the least-- the one that he's about to tell you right now-- is about how he did not become Mr. Genola in the Mr. Genola contest. Genola is a farming town, population 1,300. And the contest was a pageant for guys. There was no Miss Genola pageant. And it was a brand new tradition, only in its second year, and it was not a serious contest. Like the winner the first year, for the talent competition, ate a frozen burrito while it was still frozen. Like, that was his talent.
<v Ira Glass>他最少提及的故事——也就是他马上要讲给你们听的——是关于他在“Genola先生”比赛中是如何没能获胜的。Genola是一个农业小镇，人口1300。这个比赛是针对男性的选美。这里没有“Genola小姐”比赛。这还是一个全新的传统，才举办到第二年，而且并不是一场严肃的比赛。比如第一年的冠军，在才艺展示环节吃了一个冷冻布里托，还是没解冻的那种。这就是他的才艺。

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:48.960
<v Ira Glass>Jordan's friend, who was also named Jordan, had competed that first year wearing a blow up butterfly float during the swimsuit competition and singing Pearl Jam to his dead frog in the talent section.
<v Ira Glass>Jordan的朋友（也叫Jordan）在第一年也参加了比赛，他在泳装环节穿着充气蝴蝶浮具，在才艺展示环节对着他的死青蛙唱Pearl Jam的歌。

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:59.290
<v Jordan Riley>And he's deciding that we're going to enter the Mr. Genola content and volunteers me to be the participant, the single participant out of our house.
<v Jordan Riley>他决定我们要参加“Genola先生”比赛，并自告奋勇让我作为参赛者，也就是我们住的那栋房子里的唯一参赛者。

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:11.400
<v Ira Glass>And just so you can picture this-- they did have an audience of a couple hundred people, because it was scheduled as part of the annual Genola Day celebration. Which has a rodeo, and a greased pig chase, and a town dinner, and a parade.
<v Ira Glass>为了让你们有画面感——当时确实有几百名观众，因为它是年度Genola日庆典的一部分。庆典包括牛仔竞技表演、油腻小猪追逐赛、镇上晚宴和游行。

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:24.410
<v Jordan Riley>And in the middle of a public park, they took a bunch of wood apple bins and flipped them over, and put some plywood across it and made a stage. And then, probably eight contestants, all high schoolers--
<v Jordan Riley>在公园中央，他们把一堆木制苹果箱翻过来，铺上胶合板搭了一个舞台。大约有八名参赛者，全都是高中生——

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:31.710
<v Girl Mc>We're honored to be the MCs for the Mr. Genola pageant. We hope you understand this pageant is not a scholarship pageant.
<v Girl Mc>我们很荣幸担任“Genola先生”选美比赛的主持人。希望你们理解，这不是一场奖学金选美比赛。

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:49.140
<v Ira Glass>In the video of this, you can see two teenaged MCs dressed in spoof awards show wear-- a girl in a checkered jacket with a comically wide lapel, and a boy in formal tails and a vest, no shirt at all, bow tie, and cowboy hat. Jordan and I went right to the part where he comes on stage.
<v Ira Glass>在这段视频里，你可以看到两名十几岁的主持人穿着模仿颁奖典礼的服装——女孩穿着格纹外套，翻领宽得滑稽，男孩穿着正式的燕尾服和背心，没穿衬衫，戴着领结和牛仔帽。Jordan和我直接跳到了他上台的部分。

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:57.040
<v Boy Mc>Our fifth contestant is Jordan Alberto Riley, son of Alan and Kris Riley. We couldn't find anything special about him.
<v Boy Mc>我们的第五位选手是Jordan Alberto Riley，Alan和Kris Riley的儿子。我们没发现他有什么特别之处。

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:12.470
<v Jordan Riley>So they just announced me. And you know, it's a small town. They tell me who my parents are. And I come up on the stage, and I'm wearing a shirt with sleeves cut off, and I've got some long hair and a hat. And then I bring up a wheelchair.
<v Jordan Riley>就这样他们宣布了我。你知道，那是个小镇。他们报出了我父母的名字。我走上舞台，穿着一件袖子剪掉的衬衫，留着长发，戴着帽子。然后我推上了一把轮椅。

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:16.340
<v Jordan Riley>[CROWD CLAMORING]
<v Jordan Riley>[人群喧哗声]

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:24.290
<v Ira Glass>Jordan had used a wheelchair for a little while after an injury once. Now he starts showing off the tricks he learned then-- popping wheelies and spinning.
<v Ira Glass>Jordan曾经因伤使用过一段时间轮椅。现在他开始炫耀那时学到的技巧——翘起前轮和旋转。

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:31.180
<v Jordan Riley>I just was spinning faster and faster, until me and the wheelchair all spun off to the side, tumbling backwards.
<v Jordan Riley>我只是越转越快，直到我和轮椅一起被甩向侧面，向后翻滚。

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:37.520
<v Jordan Riley>[CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]
<v Jordan Riley>[欢呼和掌声]

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:43.580
<v Ira Glass>After a few more wheelies, Jordan gets up from the chair, flexes his muscles. Heavy metal music kicks in.
<v Ira Glass>又表演了几个翘轮动作后，Jordan从椅子上站起来，展示他的肌肉。重金属音乐响起。

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:46.240
<v Ira Glass>[HEAVY METAL MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Ira Glass>[重金属音乐播放中]

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:54.360
<v Ira Glass>And his buddies carry an old door onstage, this door that has a big pane of glass in the top third. Jordan grabs the mike to explain what's going to happen.
<v Ira Glass>他的朋友们搬来一扇旧门放在舞台上，门的上三分之一处有一块大玻璃。Jordan抢过麦克风解释即将发生什么。

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:59.380
<v Jordan Riley>I'm pretty much just breaking a window. That's about it.
<v Jordan Riley>我基本上就是打碎一扇窗户。就这么简单。

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:45.370
<v Ira Glass>Now, I'm pumping myself up by flexing and shaking my head. And I run at it and give it a good punch through the window. And then I stepped back, and I looked at this window, and there was a triangular piece of glass right in the middle of it. And I looked at that, and I thought, that's funny. I don't remember that glass having blood on it before I punched through it. And stepped back, and I'm just looking at the audience, and I looked down-- down right at my forearm, like almost to my elbow. And there was a hole. And like, in that moment in time, it was like when things go in slow motion.
<v Ira Glass>现在，我通过展示肌肉和甩头让自己兴奋起来。我冲过去，对着窗户重重地打了一拳。然后我退后一步，看着这扇窗，中间有一块三角形的玻璃碎片。我看着它，心想，真有意思。我记得我打穿它之前，玻璃上没血。我往后退，看着观众，然后低头一看——看向我的前臂，几乎直到手肘处。那里有个洞。在那一瞬间，就像一切都变成了慢动作。

00:04:45.370 --> 00:05:23.350
<v Ira Glass>I remember a severed muscle hanging out. And I'm looking at my forearm, and I think about this time that we went hiking in the Narrows of Zion's national park. And I was carrying a Rambo knife, and I went to go catch a snake. And I cut the side of this snake in his-- you can see some stuff coming out this gash, which I immediately regretted. But I stood there, and I thought about that snake as I looked at my forearm.
<v Ira Glass>我记得有一块断裂的肌肉露在外面。我看着我的前臂，想起有一次我们在锡安国家公园的窄路徒步。我带着一把兰博刀，想去抓蛇。我不小心切开了那条蛇的侧面，看到一些东西从伤口里流出来，我当时立刻就后悔了。但我站在那里，看着我的前臂时，想到了那条蛇。

00:05:23.350 --> 00:05:25.360
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:46.930
<v Jordan Riley>And then it seemed like, bam! Fast motion happens. And I turn around, and blood is just splatting on the floor. And the MC steps in. And I'm showing her my arm, and I'm saying, call an ambulance! And she says, is that real?
<v Jordan Riley>然后，砰！一切变成了快动作。我转过身，血喷溅在地板上。主持人走过来。我给她看我的手臂，说，叫救护车！她说，那是真的吗？

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:51.100
<v Ira Glass>Oh, she thought it was a prank. She thought you were just like-- you have fake blood or something.
<v Ira Glass>哦，她以为是恶作剧。她以为你只是——你用了假血之类的东西。

00:05:51.100 --> 00:06:05.080
<v Jordan Riley>Oh, totally. Everybody did. And then one of my friends tears off his t-shirt and then tourniquets around it. They're like, he's turning white. He's turning white. Just lay him down.
<v Jordan Riley>哦，完全是。每个人都这么认为。然后我一个朋友撕下他的T恤，帮我止血。他们喊着，他脸色变白了！他脸色变白了！快让他躺下。

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:23.870
<v Ira Glass>Kids start rushing the stage to see. We actually talked to one of those kids, who's grown now. And he said that he figured it was fake blood and a really cool prank. And then he got near and saw that it was real. And the next thing he knew, he was waking up under a tree with an oxygen mask. Jordan and his friends believe a few people fainted.
<v Ira Glass>孩子们开始冲向舞台观看。我们确实采访了其中一个孩子，他现在已经长大了。他说他当时以为那是假血，是一场非常酷的恶作剧。直到他走近看到那是真的。下一刻，他发现自己在树下醒来，戴着氧气面罩。Jordan和他的朋友们相信有几个人晕倒了。

00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:45.700
<v Girl Mc>Ladies and gentlemen, we're getting as cleaned up as soon as we can. Please, that happened totally on accident. I didn't know he was doing that, and we probably wouldn't have allowed it. So for the judges' information, we're going to have Mike Huell go before Clark Davis, because Mike's singing and Clark's dancing. So we can let the stage dry for a minute.
<v Girl Mc>女士们先生们，我们正尽可能清理现场。请注意，那完全是个意外。我不知道他在做那个，我们可能根本不会允许的。所以，评委们请注意，接下来是Mike Huell，然后是Clark Davis，因为Mike要唱歌，Clark要跳舞。我们可以让舞台晾干一会儿。

00:06:45.700 --> 00:07:08.500
<v Ira Glass>Jordan says that this girl MC, later, said how mad she was that he ruined her pageant. But at this moment, she and the other teenaged MC had to ad-lib their way through this situation that I think even a very experienced public speaker would find challenging. There's a man on stage with them mopping up blood-- like with a mop, there's so much blood. And they have to fill time.
<v Ira Glass>Jordan说那个女主持人后来表示，她非常生气他毁了她的选美。但在那一刻，她和另一位少年主持人不得不临场发挥，应对这种我认为即使是非常有经验的公共演讲者也会觉得极具挑战性的情况。舞台上有一个人正在用拖把清理血迹——真的是用拖把，血太多了。他们必须填补时间。

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:12.820
<v Boy Mc>All right. Um, well, how's the show so far?
<v Boy Mc>好的。呃，那么，目前的表演感觉如何？

00:07:12.820 --> 00:07:18.820
<v Boy Mc>[LAUGHTER AND CHEERING]
<v Boy Mc>[笑声和欢呼声]

00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:28.250
<v Ira Glass>That goes over surprisingly well. And so to stall for time, they run through what apparently is the only standby material they have. The boy onstage pulls out a fistful of pages.
<v Ira Glass>效果好得令人惊讶。为了拖延时间，他们用了显然是他们唯一的备用稿。台上的男孩掏出一把纸。

00:07:28.250 --> 00:07:49.180
<v Boy Mc>All right. Here's something a man named John put on his answering machine. It says, hi, this is John. If you are the phone company, I already sent the money. If you are my parents, please send money. If you are my friends, you owe me money. If you are a female, don't worry. I have plenty of money.
<v Boy Mc>好的。这是一位名叫John的人留在他电话留言机上的内容。他说，嗨，我是John。如果你是电话公司，我已经寄钱了。如果你是我父母，请寄钱给我。如果你是我朋友，你欠我钱。如果你是女性，别担心。我有很多钱。

00:07:49.180 --> 00:07:53.990
<v Boy Mc>[LAUGHTER]
<v Boy Mc>[笑声]

00:07:53.990 --> 00:07:59.280
<v Boy Mc>Here's another one. Hi, I am probably home. I just-- I'm just--
<v Boy Mc>还有这一条。嗨，我可能在家。我只是——我只是——

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:32.800
<v Ira Glass>They never did another Mr. Genola contest. This pretty much killed it off. The ambulance came. Jordan didn't lose an arm. He's fine. He's in his 30s now. And the phrase that he uses for what he's become now is "contributing member of society." He settled down. He's a farmer-- grows apples, cherries, peaches-- with a family, a young daughter. He hasn't lived in Genola since that summer. But now and then-- this just happened at a farm stand a month ago-- he runs into people who say to him, aren't you the guy who almost cut off his arm in that pageant? That's what they know him for.
<v Ira Glass>他们再也没有举办过Genola先生比赛。这件事基本上让它夭折了。救护车来了。Jordan没有丢掉手臂。他没事。他现在30多岁了。他用“社会贡献者”这个词来形容现在的自己。他安定下来了。他是个农民——种苹果、樱桃、桃子——有家庭，有一个年幼的女儿。从那个夏天起他就再没住在Genola。但偶尔——就在一个月前在一个农产品摊位上——他会遇到人们对他说，你就是那个在选美比赛中差点切断手臂的家伙吗？这就是人们认识他的原因。

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:37.510
<v Jordan Riley>Well, in the town of Genola, that's my badge to wear.
<v Jordan Riley>嗯，在Genola镇，这就是我的勋章。

00:08:37.510 --> 00:08:38.450
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:08:38.450 --> 00:08:48.170
<v Jordan Riley>They know me as the guy who did the Mr. Genola pageant.
<v Jordan Riley>他们认识我作为那个参加Genola先生选美的人。

00:08:48.170 --> 00:09:02.820
<v Ira Glass>It's been over a decade, and he still doesn't ever watch this video. Too soon. Still makes him wince. I asked him when he will be able to watch this and not wince, and he said he thinks it pretty much always will be too soon for this one.
<v Ira Glass>十多年过去了，他仍然从来不看这段视频。为时过早。还是会让他退缩。我问他什么时候能看这段视频而不感到退缩，他说他觉得这一辈子对这个视频来说都太早了。

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:19.830
<v Jordan Riley>Yeah. Like I said, this was not the proudest story. And just seeing us at that time, in those teenage-- it's just painful to watch. I'm not a fan of that guy.
<v Jordan Riley>是的。就像我说的，这并不是我最自豪的故事。看着当时的我们，在那十几岁的年纪——看那个真的很痛苦。我不喜欢那个家伙。

00:09:19.830 --> 00:09:47.660
<v Ira Glass>Today on our radio program-- "Too Soon." Sometimes it will always be too soon. But sometimes, you can imagine a world where the thing that you're remembering really will become much less of a big deal, and you'll be able to think about it without cringing. If you're lucky, maybe it'll even start to seem funny. We have two stories for you today. In each of them, there's a piece of video that is capturing a moment. And in each of them, the past crashes against the present in this way that is really kind of mesmerizing.
<v Ira Glass>今天在我们的广播节目中——“太早了”。有时它永远都会是太早。但有时，你可以想象一个世界，你记忆中的那件事真的变得没那么重要了，你可以不用退缩地回想它。如果幸运的话，它甚至可能会开始变得好笑。今天我们有两个故事要讲给你们听。在每个故事中，都有一段捕捉瞬间的视频。在每个故事中，过去以一种非常迷人的方式与现在碰撞。

00:09:47.660 --> 00:09:59.430
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>这里是芝加哥WBEZ电台，《美国生活》(This American Life)。我是Ira Glass。请继续收听。

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:17.780
<v Ira Glass>So Jordan's story was about a prank gone wrong. And you say this next story is like that, too. Except, in this next story, it is not just one prank. And "wrong" does not capture the sheer wrongness of what you're about to hear. Nancy Updike tells the story.
<v Ira Glass>所以Jordan的故事是关于一个弄巧成拙的恶作剧。你说这下一个故事也差不多。只是，在下一个故事中，不仅仅是一个恶作剧。而且“弄巧成拙”这个词根本无法形容你即将听到的事情的荒谬程度。Nancy Updike来讲这个故事。

00:10:17.780 --> 00:10:36.060
<v Ira Glass>And I should say, before we start-- if you're listening to this on our podcast or online, we have unbeeped some curse words in the internet version of this story. If you want to listen with your kids in the car, or you just don't like curse words, there's a beeped version available at our website, thisamericanlife.org. OK. Here's Nancy.
<v Ira Glass>我得说一下，在我们开始之前——如果你是在我们的播客或网上收听，我们在网络版本中保留了原本的粗俗语言。如果你想和孩子在车里收听，或者你不喜欢粗俗语言，我们的网站 thisamericanlife.org 上有消音版本。好的。请听Nancy讲述。

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:57.370
<v Nancy Updike>Harmon Leon is a writer and comedian whose cocktail party story about "the weirdest gig I ever did" is a more weird, by a lot, than anyone else's that I've heard. He answered an ad for a job several years ago that called for a comedian who would be the hilarious sidekick to a celebrity on a new, hidden camera prank TV show.
<v Nancy Updike>Harmon Leon是一位作家和喜剧演员，他关于“我做过最诡异的工作”的酒会故事，比我听过的任何人的都要诡异得多。几年前，他应聘了一份工作，招聘要求是找一名喜剧演员，在一部全新的隐藏摄像机恶作剧电视节目中担任名人的搞笑搭档。

00:10:57.370 --> 00:11:11.510
<v Harmon Leon>It was on the Los Angeles Craigslist ad. And I'm thinking, oh, it's a celebrity hidden camera show. I've done a bunch of prank stuff. So I sent him my prank reel. I got the job, and they said they couldn't disclose who the celebrity was.
<v Harmon Leon>那是在洛杉矶的Craigslist广告上看到的。我想，哦，这是一个名人隐藏摄像机节目。我做过很多恶作剧节目。所以我寄去了我的恶作剧样片。我得到了那份工作，他们说不能透露那位名人是谁。

00:11:11.510 --> 00:11:12.810
<v Nancy Updike>Harmon rolled his eyes.
<v Nancy Updike>Harmon翻了个白眼。

00:11:12.810 --> 00:11:22.220
<v Harmon Leon>Well, just people say that as like, oh, when we tell you who the celebrity is, you're not going to believe it. You're not going to believe it. And your mind just goes, you know, you're jaded--
<v Harmon Leon>嗯，人们总会那样说，比如，哦，当我们告诉你名人是谁时，你不会相信的。你绝对不会相信。你的大脑只会想，你知道，你已经麻木了——

00:11:22.220 --> 00:11:23.570
<v Nancy Updike>Eh, I'll probably believe it, you think.
<v Nancy Updike>嗯，我想我可能会相信的，你会想。

