00:20:16.630 --> 00:20:42.800
<v Nancy Updike>I told Harmon that I figured, as I was watching the show, that the producers had just edited out any moments where people got annoyed or uncomfortable when they got juiced, or pulled away when O.J. went to hug them, or recoiled. And I was completely wrong about that. Harmon told me that during the two weeks of filming, as far as he could remember, no one reacted that way. Other people on the set told me the same thing. There were no moments like that to cut out.
<v Nancy Updike>我告诉Harmon，我在看节目时想，制片人肯定是剪掉了人们感到烦躁或不舒服、当人们被Juice时避开，或者当O.J.去拥抱他们时后退的时刻。我完全错了。Harmon告诉我，在两周的拍摄过程中，据他所知，没有人那样反应。片场的其他人也告诉了我同样的事情。没有那样的时刻需要剪掉。

00:20:42.800 --> 00:21:01.020
<v Harmon Leon>I thought, oh, man, there's going to be like just outraged people, or people freaking out. And that was actually the most mind numbing part about the two-week production, was that people just were actually thrilled when they find out they'd been juiced by O.J.
<v Harmon Leon>我以为，哦，天哪，肯定会有愤怒的人，或者崩溃的人。而这其实是那两周拍摄中最令人麻木的部分，就是人们在发现自己被O.J.给Juice后，实际上感到非常激动。

00:21:01.020 --> 00:21:02.310
<v Woman>I got juiced!
<v Woman>我被Juiced了！

00:21:02.310 --> 00:21:03.830
<v Woman>I've been juiced.
<v Woman>我被Juiced了。

00:21:03.830 --> 00:21:08.930
<v Man>(SINGING) I've been juiced.
<v Man>（唱歌）我被Juiced了。

00:21:08.930 --> 00:21:10.050
<v Man>[LAUGHTER]
<v Man>[笑声]

00:21:10.050 --> 00:21:37.440
<v Harmon Leon>You know, like we were in Las Vegas filming, and people would just swarm in off the streets to get photos with O.J. Like mothers and daughters requesting photos. This kid, he started rapping for O.J. And then he high fives his friend, and he goes, I just rapped for O.J., man. That's as big as it gets! And it was like, they would just be laughing. It was like, whoa-o-o. Hope he doesn't kill anyone while we're here! Ha ha ha.
<v Harmon Leon>你知道，就像我们在拉斯维加斯拍摄时，人们会从街上蜂拥而至，和O.J.合影。就像母亲和女儿要求合影。有个孩子，他开始为O.J.即兴说唱。然后他和他朋友击掌，说，我刚才为O.J.说唱了，伙计。这简直太棒了！就像是，他们只会笑。就像是，哇喔。希望我们在这儿的时候他没杀任何人！哈哈哈。

00:21:37.440 --> 00:21:39.600
<v Nancy Updike>Did anyone say anything like that?
<v Nancy Updike>有人说过那样的话吗？

00:21:39.600 --> 00:22:00.280
<v Harmon Leon>I would hear people off to the side saying that, but they wouldn't say it like angry. They would say it like, oh, he's just funny O.J. who-- he just like became like-- he's like this funny cartoon character. He's like the Santa Claus of murder.
<v Harmon Leon>我听到旁边的人那样说，但他们不会生气地那样说。他们会说，哦，他只是有趣的O.J.，他变成了像——他变成了像这样一个有趣的卡通人物。他就像谋杀界的圣诞老人。

00:22:00.280 --> 00:22:32.030
<v Nancy Updike>Whatever charisma was working in person doesn't come across on the screen. In fact, let me step briefly outside Harmon's story of what it's like to make Juiced to talk more about what it's like to watch Juiced. I've shown this now to so many people, and the sheer scope of the chaos in the show is disorienting. Not just to me. Watching the show feels like being winked at from across the room by someone who may be flirting with you, or may be messing with you, or may just have a tic. It's not clear what to make of anything that's happening.
<v Nancy Updike>无论现场有什么个人魅力，在屏幕上都体现不出来。事实上，让我稍微跳出Harmon讲述的《Juiced》制作过程，更多地谈谈观看《Juiced》的感觉。我现在给很多人看过这个，节目中混乱的范围令人不知所措。不仅仅是对我。看这个节目感觉就像有人从房间另一头对你挤眉弄眼，那个人可能是在调情，可能是在捉弄你，或者可能只是抽动。不知道该如何理解正在发生的事情。

00:22:32.030 --> 00:22:38.580
<v Nancy Updike>In between the pranks in Juiced , there's a scene-changing package that includes, for some reason, a gunshot sound effect--
<v Nancy Updike>在《Juiced》的恶作剧之间，有一个换场包，出于某种原因，包含了一个枪声效果——

00:22:38.580 --> 00:22:39.980
<v Nancy Updike>[GUN SHOTS]
<v Nancy Updike>[枪声]

00:22:39.980 --> 00:22:53.690
<v Ira Glass>--whatever that's supposed to mean, or not mean. And 20 to 30 seconds of music, during which O.J. is surrounded by strippers. Sometimes he's rapping, dressed as a pimp-- big fuzzy black hat, zebra striped lapels.
<v Ira Glass>——那意味着什么，或者不意味着什么。以及20到30秒的音乐，期间O.J.被脱衣舞女包围。有时他在说唱，打扮成皮条客——戴着巨大的黑色毛绒帽子，斑马条纹翻领。

00:22:53.690 --> 00:23:04.290
<v O.J. Simpson>(RAPPING) A lot of people wonder about my intentions. Why do people ask me so many questions about how I made it to the top, about all the times I made those people [INAUDIBLE].
<v O.J. Simpson>（说唱）很多人好奇我的意图。为什么人们问我那么多关于我是如何爬到顶端的问题，关于我让那些人[听不清]的所有那些时刻。

00:23:04.290 --> 00:23:31.930
<v Nancy Updike>There's pole dancing. They give him lap dances. And again, this scene is in between nearly every prank. And he's surrounded by dancing women who mostly have long, blond hair and don't not resemble his dead ex-wife. And he's mugging to the camera with that expression, like, how great is this? But before you can decide what that means, if anything, besides just the demographics of who was available to shoot this scene, it's on to the next puzzling moment.
<v Nancy Updike>有钢管舞。她们给他跳膝上舞。再说一次，这个场景几乎在每个恶作剧之间。他被跳舞的女性包围，她们大多有着长长的金发，长得不不像他死去的那个前妻。他对着摄像机做着表情，好像在说，这难道不棒吗？但在你决定这意味什么之前，如果有什么意义的话，除了当时谁有空拍摄这个场景的人口统计学数据，节目又进入了下一个令人费解的时刻。

00:23:31.930 --> 00:23:41.250
<v Nancy Updike>For instance, in the open house prank. At one point, Harmon, playing the angry homeowner whose house is being looked at, tries to blame O.J. for breaking one of the vases.
<v Nancy Updike>例如，在房屋开放日的恶作剧。有一次，Harmon扮演那个被看房的愤怒房主，试图把花瓶破碎归咎于O.J.。

00:23:41.250 --> 00:23:46.450
<v Harmon Leon>Did you do this? Can you just pay for my vase, please? Can you just pay for my vase?
<v Harmon Leon>是你干的吗？你能赔我的花瓶吗，求你了？你能赔我的花瓶吗？

00:23:46.450 --> 00:23:51.960
<v O.J. Simpson>Sir, I wasn't around. I get blamed for nothing else. All right, in my life, I've been blamed for enough shit.
<v O.J. Simpson>先生，我不在附近。我无缘无故被指责。好吧，在我的人生中，我已经背够了黑锅。

00:23:51.960 --> 00:23:53.360
<v O.J. Simpson>[VASE SHATTERING]
<v O.J. Simpson>[花瓶破碎声]

00:23:53.360 --> 00:24:21.450
<v Nancy Updike>When O.J. Says, "I've been blamed for enough shit," he sounds genuinely angry. Which means, I think, he's mad at being fake blamed for breaking a vase in a prank, because it reminds him that he was on trial for murder 10 years ago. There's a prank where O.J. pretends to be having an affair with another man's wife, and the man gets really angry. There's a prank where O.J. plays himself, but a homeless version of himself, selling bags of oranges by the side of the road.
<v Nancy Updike>当O.J.说，“我已经背够了黑锅”时，他听起来非常愤怒。这意味着，我认为，他因为在恶作剧中被假装指责弄碎花瓶而感到愤怒，因为它提醒了他10年前他因为谋杀案受审的事情。有个恶作剧是O.J.假装和别人的妻子有染，那个人非常生气。有个恶作剧是O.J.扮演自己，但是一个无家可归版本的自己，在路边卖橘子袋。

00:24:21.450 --> 00:24:24.760
<v O.J. Simpson>Oranges from O.J.! OJ right here!
<v O.J. Simpson>O.J.的橘子！就是O.J.就在这儿！

00:24:24.760 --> 00:24:46.450
<v Nancy Updike>Is that an edgy comment on his fallen status, or just another, look at what this celebrity is willing to do on our reality show moment? No time to think. The show plows onward to smaller moments of just raw unlikableness. In one prank, O.J. is dressed as an employee of a fast food place, and he's working the drive-through window. A woman pulls up in her 40s, maybe early 50s.
<v Nancy Updike>这是一个对他地位跌落的尖锐评论，还是只是又一个“看这个名人在我们的真人秀节目中愿意做什么”的时刻？没时间思考。节目继续推进到更多纯粹令人不悦的小时刻。在一个恶作剧里，O.J.打扮成一家快餐店的员工，在汽车穿梭窗口工作。一个40岁左右，可能50出头的女人开车过来。

00:24:46.450 --> 00:24:49.750
<v Woman>Large fries, large orange juice.
<v Woman>大份薯条，大份橘子汁。

00:24:49.750 --> 00:24:55.780
<v O.J. Simpson>You sure you want those large fries? You know what they're saying about fast food making you fat.
<v O.J. Simpson>你确定要那些大份薯条吗？你知道人们是怎么说快餐让你变胖的。

00:24:55.780 --> 00:25:15.730
<v Nancy Updike>The women who's ordering is overweight, so that's the joke. She's fat. She's ordering fries. In Juiced , O.J. has plunged himself into reality TV, a genre that has no allegiance to the idea that the star has to come out looking good. But there's something off about this show that goes beyond that.
<v Nancy Updike>那个点餐的女人超重，所以这就是笑点。她很胖。她在点薯条。在《Juiced》中，O.J.把自己沉浸在真人秀中，这个流派对明星必须表现出良好形象的观点没有任何忠诚度。但这个节目确实有些不对劲，超出了这一点。

00:25:15.730 --> 00:25:34.910
<v Nancy Updike>For all the frenzy on the screen, all the invitations to gawk, or be titillated, or outraged, or just shake your head in wonder, mostly it felt empty. It's tiring to watch, strangely tiring.
<v Nancy Updike>尽管屏幕上充满了狂热，所有的邀请去围观，去被刺激，去被激怒，或者只是摇着头感到困惑，但大部分时间它感觉是空的。看起来很累，奇怪地累人。

00:25:34.910 --> 00:26:00.800
<v Nancy Updike>When I talked to Harmon, he said that during the filming, there were moments unlike any other reality show he'd worked on, unsurprisingly. For starters, as far as he could tell, everyone had been told the same thing he had-- don't bring up the murders or anything related to them. And no one did, except O.J. Before one prank, O.J. was being made up to look like an 81-year-old white man. The makeup took three hours to apply.
<v Nancy Updike>当我和Harmon交谈时，他说在拍摄期间，确实有一些时刻与他参与过的任何其他真人秀都不同，这并不奇怪。首先，据他所知，每个人都被告知了同样的事情——不要提起谋杀案或与之相关的任何事情。没人提起，除了O.J.。在一个恶作剧之前，O.J.被化妆成一个81岁的白人老头。化妆过程花了三个小时。

00:26:00.800 --> 00:26:08.630
<v Harmon Leon>While he's getting the makeup applied, he has the TV turned to Court TV. You know, and I was like, don't mention O.J., Court TV. Don't mention O.J.
<v Harmon Leon>当他化妆的时候，他把电视转到了Court TV（法庭频道）。你知道，我就像，别提到O.J.，Court TV。别提到O.J.。

00:26:08.630 --> 00:26:09.650
<v Nancy Updike>He's saying this to the TV?
<v Nancy Updike>他是对着电视说这个吗？

00:26:09.650 --> 00:26:26.660
<v Harmon Leon>No, no. I'm saying this to myself. Like, oh Court TV, please don't mention O.J. Because I don't know how he would react. But he was actually just like-- he kind of wanted them to mention him. He was like, how are they going to work me into this, you know? You know, during my trial, my lawyers watched my back. You know?
<v Harmon Leon>不，不。我是对自己说。就像，哦Court TV，请别提到O.J.。因为我不知道他会如何反应。但他其实只是——他有点希望他们提到他。他说，他们会怎么把我加到这个里面，你知道吗？你知道，在我审判期间，我的律师守着我的背。你知道吗？

00:26:26.660 --> 00:26:30.100
<v Nancy Updike>So he would-- OK. So it was sort of reminiscing.
<v Nancy Updike>所以他会——好的。所以那是一种回忆。

00:26:30.100 --> 00:26:30.720
<v Harmon Leon>Yeah, yeah.
<v Harmon Leon>是的，是的。

00:26:30.720 --> 00:26:32.640
<v Nancy Updike>While the women are putting the makeup on him.
<v Nancy Updike>当女人们在他脸上化妆时。

00:26:32.640 --> 00:26:39.170
<v Harmon Leon>Yep. He said he really liked watching Court TV.
<v Harmon Leon>是的。他说他真的很喜欢看Court TV。

00:26:39.170 --> 00:26:48.160
<v Harmon Leon>Again, when he was getting the makeup applied, he would actually tell O.J. jokes. So we had the pleasure of hearing like O.J. Jokes from O.J. himself.
<v Harmon Leon>再说一次，当他化妆时，他甚至会自己讲O.J.的笑话。所以我们有幸听到了O.J.亲自讲O.J.的笑话。

00:26:48.160 --> 00:26:49.650
<v Nancy Updike>What's an O.J. joke?
<v Nancy Updike>什么是O.J.笑话？

00:26:49.650 --> 00:26:59.700
<v Harmon Leon>He actually said this. He goes, who's the first Jewish guy to get a Heisman Trophy? Fred Goldman, because he's got mine.
<v Harmon Leon>他竟然说了这个。他说，第一个拿到海斯曼奖杯的犹太人是谁？Fred Goldman，因为他拿走了我的。

00:26:59.700 --> 00:27:02.090
<v Nancy Updike>Because the Goldmans brought a civil suit against him that--
<v Nancy Updike>因为Goldman家对他提起了民事诉讼，那——

00:27:02.090 --> 00:27:03.470
<v Harmon Leon>Yeah, and took his--
<v Harmon Leon>是的，拿走了他的——

00:27:03.470 --> 00:27:03.830
<v Nancy Updike>Money.
<v Nancy Updike>钱。

00:27:03.830 --> 00:27:18.020
<v Harmon Leon>Heisman. And so, you know, everyone just kind of uncomfortably just shifted around and looked at their shoes and contemplated career choices.
<v Harmon Leon>海斯曼奖杯。所以，你知道，每个人都尴尬地挪动着，看着自己的鞋子，思考职业选择。

00:27:18.020 --> 00:27:36.140
<v Nancy Updike>The cinematographer of Juiced , Luc Nicknair, told me there was a split in the crew. Some people thought, look, this man was a great athlete and a big star. And he was acquitted. And that's that, and that's how I see this. And others who thought, yeah, he was acquitted, but I still have other thoughts about what may have happened.
<v Nancy Updike>《Juiced》的摄影师Luc Nicknair告诉我，摄制组内部存在分裂。有些人认为，听着，这个男人是一位伟大的运动员和巨星。他被判无罪了。就是这样，我就是这么看待的。其他人则认为，是的，他被判无罪了，但我对可能发生的事情仍有其他想法。

00:27:36.140 --> 00:27:38.030
<v Luc Nicknair>Definitely two schools of thought.
<v Luc Nicknair>确实有两种思想流派。

00:27:38.030 --> 00:27:38.650
<v Nancy Updike>Uh-huh.
<v Nancy Updike>嗯哼。

00:27:38.650 --> 00:27:40.560
<v Luc Nicknair>Yeah. There are definitely two schools of thought.
<v Luc Nicknair>是的。确实有两种思想流派。

00:27:40.560 --> 00:27:42.220
<v Nancy Updike>And would people argue about it?
<v Nancy Updike>人们会对此争论吗？

00:27:42.220 --> 00:28:00.480
<v Luc Nicknair>He talked about it privately. Actually, at the end of the shoot, this was the really messed up thing. They decided to shoot interviews with everyone of the crew about our experiences. And then, at the end of the interview, they would say, guilty or not guilty?
<v Luc Nicknair>他私下里谈过这个。事实上，在拍摄结束时，这是一件非常糟糕的事情。他们决定拍摄对所有摄制组人员关于我们经历的采访。然后，在采访结束时，他们会问，有罪还是无罪？

00:28:00.480 --> 00:28:01.330
<v Nancy Updike>Oh my God.
<v Nancy Updike>哦，天哪。

00:28:01.330 --> 00:28:06.230
<v Luc Nicknair>That did not get on the DVD. None of that got on the DVD.
<v Luc Nicknair>那部分没上DVD。那些都没上DVD。

00:28:06.230 --> 00:28:06.760
<v Nancy Updike>And what was--
<v Nancy Updike>那是——

00:28:06.760 --> 00:28:09.670
<v Luc Nicknair>I'm on camera myself.
<v Luc Nicknair>我自己就在摄像机前。

00:28:09.670 --> 00:28:11.190
<v Nancy Updike>What did you say?
<v Nancy Updike>你说了什么？

00:28:11.190 --> 00:28:12.410
<v Luc Nicknair>Guilty.
<v Luc Nicknair>有罪。

00:28:12.410 --> 00:28:14.120
<v Nancy Updike>And is that what you thought going in?
<v Nancy Updike>那就是你进去时的想法吗？

00:28:14.120 --> 00:28:33.560
<v Luc Nicknair>I didn't know what to think. That's why I did the job. You know, I secretly want to know. It's like-- and here I was going to be given an opportunity to go and shoot this deranged project, and I decided to do the gig.
<v Luc Nicknair>我不知道该想什么。这就是为什么我做那份工作。你知道，我私下里想知道。就像——而这里我将获得机会去拍摄这个疯狂的项目，我决定接这个工作。

00:28:33.560 --> 00:28:51.890
<v Luc Nicknair>I mean, I thought about that when I was involved with it. I thought about that. It's like, people are going to study this project. And all of a sudden, you called me 10 years later. [LAUGHS] You know?
<v Luc Nicknair>我是说，当我参与其中时我也想过。我思考过。就像，人们会研究这个项目。然后突然间，你10年后给我打电话。[笑] 你知道吗？

00:28:51.890 --> 00:29:00.700
<v Nancy Updike>Harmon, and Luc also, said Las Vegas, where they did the second week of shooting, is where things really turned sour and dark.
<v Nancy Updike>Harmon和Luc也说，拉斯维加斯，他们拍摄第二周的地方，事情真的变得酸涩和黑暗。

00:29:00.700 --> 00:29:31.130
<v Harmon Leon>They just seemed to sort of give up on ideas. And O.J. just got less and less interested in it. He didn't show up. And then we showed up, he was just like really drunk. Like O.J. was supposed to play a wacky motel clerk. You know? Like look out, here comes wacky motel clerk O.J.! You know, that's the premise of that gag. But he was just like completely drunk, and that was the first time they just sort of propped him up in the corner, like on a stool, and just let him be sort of near the pranks.
<v Harmon Leon>他们似乎放弃了想法。O.J.也变得越来越不感兴趣。他不出现。然后我们出现，他只是喝得很醉。比如O.J.本应扮演一个怪异的汽车旅馆前台。你知道吗？就像当心，怪异的汽车旅馆前台O.J.来了！你知道，那是那个恶作剧的前提。但他只是完全喝醉了，那是他们第一次把他在角落里撑起来，比如坐在凳子上，只让他待在恶作剧附近。

00:29:31.130 --> 00:29:42.800
<v Nancy Updike>Harmon said he, Harmon, ended up playing the wacky motel clerk. And sometimes, he even had to be the one to tell people, you've been juiced! Whatever that means, when the person doing it is not O.J. Simpson.
<v Nancy Updike>Harmon说他，Harmon，最后扮演了怪异的汽车旅馆前台。有时，他甚至不得不成为那个告诉人们“你被Juiced了！”的人。无论那是意味着什么，当做那件事的人不是O.J. Simpson时。

00:29:42.800 --> 00:29:56.770
<v Harmon Leon>After I juice a couple, O.J.'s in the corner, drunk. And he would lean into the couple, go, hey, I'm O.J. Hey, do you recognize me?
<v Harmon Leon>在我Juiced了一对夫妇后，O.J.在角落里，喝醉了。他会凑向那对夫妇，说，嘿，我是O.J.。嘿，你认出我了吗？

00:29:56.770 --> 00:30:32.940
<v Nancy Updike>O.J. Simpson's lawyer, who handles media requests, didn't respond to my emails and phone calls about Juiced. I did reach the executive producer of Juiced, Rick Mahr. We talked on the phone-- after months of unanswered voicemails, texts, and emails from us, then, out of the blue, a legal document from him, just a little heads up. Then a series of very friendly but off the record conversations, mixed in with two taped interviews that he only agreed to if he could approve every quote. So here's what he approved.
<v Nancy Updike>O.J. Simpson的律师，处理媒体请求的人，没有回复我关于《Juiced》的电子邮件和电话。我确实联系到了《Juiced》的执行制片人Rick Mahr。我们在电话里谈了——在几个月无人接听的语音信箱、短信和电子邮件之后，突然，他发来一份法律文件，只是一个小小的提醒。接着是一系列非常友好但非正式的谈话，夹杂着两次录音采访，他只同意在他能批准每一个引语的情况下进行。所以这是他批准的内容。

00:30:32.940 --> 00:30:38.580
<v Nancy Updike>He said, "I had it all wrong when I called Juiced the worst celebrity comeback vehicle ever made."
<v Nancy Updike>他说，“当我称《Juiced》为史上最差的名人复出工具时，我完全错了。”

00:30:38.580 --> 00:30:59.650
<v Rick Mahr>It was never meant to be a comeback vehicle for O.J. or try to turn him into a mainstream star again. We were tasked with creating a reality show that cut through the clutter, that everybody would be talking about, really during an era when there was a reality TV boom, and there was a lot of one upsmanship in the marketplace.
<v Rick Mahr>它从来不是为了O.J.复出而设计的，也不是试图让他再次成为主流明星。我们的任务是制作一个突破混乱的真人秀，每个人都会谈论它，确实是在一个真人秀繁荣的时代，市场上有大量的竞争。

00:30:59.650 --> 00:31:21.700
<v Nancy Updike>He said the goal was just to let the cameras run, whether O.J. looked good or not. He said the show being a pile on of one thing after another that may or may not make sense-- the blond strippers, the dead-end pranks, the weird little verite moments-- that's not a fumble. It's exactly what they were going for. Their business model was get people talking, so pile it on.
<v Nancy Updike>他说目标只是让摄像机运行，不管O.J.看起来好不好。他说这个节目是一堆接一堆可能或可能没有意义的事情——金发脱衣舞女、死胡同的恶作剧、奇怪的小真实时刻——那不是失误。这正是他们追求的。他们的商业模式是让人谈论，所以堆上去吧。

00:31:21.700 --> 00:31:27.370
<v Nancy Updike>He said, look, this wasn't designed to win Emmys. It's supposed to be a nonstop barrage of craziness.
<v Nancy Updike>他说，看，这不是为了赢艾美奖而设计的。它应该是一阵不停的疯狂轰炸。

00:31:27.370 --> 00:31:33.340
<v Rick Mahr>You know, I'd be the first to say it. It's not everybody's-- it's not everybody's cup of tea.
<v Rick Mahr>你知道，我会第一个说出来的。这不是每个人的——这不是每个人的菜。

00:31:33.340 --> 00:31:45.080
<v Nancy Updike>Nielson, the ratings organization, told me the Juiced DVD sold fewer than 100 copies, one of which I own. Rick told me, yeah. He buried the project on purpose.
<v Nancy Updike>尼尔森，收视率组织，告诉我《Juiced》DVD的销量不到100份，其中一张归我所有。Rick告诉我，是的。他故意埋葬了这个项目。

00:31:45.080 --> 00:32:06.990
<v Rick Mahr>We had the blinders on to make one crazy reality TV show. After we did it-- and I think we did that-- after we did it, there was a part of me that said, what the hell did we just do?
<v Rick Mahr>我们戴着眼罩去制作一个疯狂的真人秀。在我们做完之后——而且我认为我们做到了——在我们做完之后，我的一部分说，我们到底做了什么？

00:32:06.990 --> 00:32:18.790
<v Nancy Updike>Juiced was coming out around the same time that O.J. released a book called If I Did It . Huge outcry. The woman who was publishing the book lost her job. A two-hour Fox special was canceled.
<v Nancy Updike>《Juiced》在O.J.出版一本名为《If I Did It》（如果我做了）的书的同时推出。引起了巨大的强烈抗议。那名出版这本书的女人丢了工作。福克斯的两个小时特别节目被取消了。

00:32:18.790 --> 00:32:27.720
<v Rick Mahr>And we pulled it. We pulled it from circulation. And it's been gathering dust until now.
<v Rick Mahr>我们撤下了它。我们把它从流通中撤下了。直到现在它还在积灰。

00:32:27.720 --> 00:32:31.810
<v Nancy Updike>Yeah. You were surprised when I told you that I had a copy.
<v Nancy Updike>是的。当我告诉你我有副本时你很惊讶。

00:32:31.810 --> 00:32:33.460
<v Rick Mahr>I was.
<v Rick Mahr>我很惊讶。

00:32:33.460 --> 00:33:00.400
<v Nancy Updike>Rick is now re-releasing the Juiced material with footage that's never been seen and an interview with O.J. From the time. It's called O.J. Un gloved , the Lost Tapes . He's got the domain name ojsimpson.com. Makes sense-- the 20th anniversary of the murder trial verdict is coming up this fall. There's a multipart FX miniseries in the works. Cuba Gooding Junior is playing O.J. They filmed the Bronco scene a few weeks ago.
<v Nancy Updike>Rick现在正在重新发布《Juiced》的材料，包括从未见过的片段和对O.J.的采访。是从那个时候开始的。它叫《O.J. Ungloved, the Lost Tapes》。他有域名 ojsimpson.com。有道理——谋杀案审判判决20周年纪念即将来临。FX正在制作一部多集迷你剧。Cuba Gooding Jr.扮演O.J.。几周前他们拍摄了Bronco场景。

00:33:00.400 --> 00:33:40.750
<v Nancy Updike>Rick didn't want to say if Juiced was O.J.'s idea or not. When I asked if O.J. was paid for it, he said, O.J. did this because he wanted to do it. He said his understanding is that O.J. did see Juiced , every frame, and approved it before it went out. When I asked Rick if O.J. had ever said, look, this is how I want to be portrayed in this show, Rick said the answers to what O.J. wanted out of Juiced , or why he did it in the first place, are locked in O.J.'s head. All he, Rick, could say is how unworried O.J. seemed to him while they were filming, like anyway they put the footage together would be great, because it was O.J.
<v Nancy Updike>Rick不想说《Juiced》是不是O.J.的主意。当我问O.J.是否为此得到报酬时，他说，O.J.做这个是因为他想做。他说他的理解是，O.J.确实看了《Juiced》，每一帧，并在它发布前批准了。当我问Rick O.J.是否曾经说过，看，这就是我想在这个节目中被描绘的方式，Rick说关于O.J.想从《Juiced》中得到什么，或者他起初为什么这么做的答案，都锁在O.J.的脑袋里。他，Rick，唯一能说的是，在拍摄时O.J.看起来多么不担心，好像他们把片段拼在一起的方式都会很棒，因为那是O.J.。

00:33:40.750 --> 00:33:53.060
<v Rick Mahr>I think history has shown that O.J. Simpson is his own boss. And O.J. is going to do what O.J. is going to do.
<v Rick Mahr>我认为历史表明O.J. Simpson是他自己的老板。O.J.会做他想做的事。

