00:34:47.200 --> 00:35:28.320
<v Nancy Updike>I bet that, to a very famous person, losing the love of the general public feels the way aging can feel-- an unfair changing of the rules, too big to accept. And so maybe it's not that surprising that O.J. put himself in a prank show 10 years after the trial, basically saying, remember me? Still charming. Watching Juiced sometimes felt like seeing a beautiful older actress trying to reprise the exact role that had made her famous when she was younger, as though nothing has changed.
<v Nancy Updike>我敢打赌，对于一个非常有名的人来说，失去公众的喜爱感觉就像衰老一样——一种不公平的规则改变，大到无法接受。所以也许O.J.在审判10年后把自己放进一个恶作剧节目并不奇怪，基本上是在说，还记得我吗？还是很迷人。看《Juiced》有时感觉就像看到一个美丽的年长女演员试图重演她年轻时让她成名的角色，好像什么都没变一样。

00:35:28.320 --> 00:35:52.650
<v Nancy Updike>Remember earlier I talked about Juiced feeling empty? How the show keeps urging viewers to be scandalized and outraged, but mostly I just felt tired watching it? I think I figured out why while I was watching and rewatching this last scene I want to play for you, the most stunning one in Juiced. It starts with O.J. explaining the prank that's about to happen, which takes place at a used car lot.
<v Nancy Updike>记得之前我谈到《Juiced》感觉很空吗？节目如何不断敦促观众去感到丑闻和愤怒，但我看的时候大部分只觉得累？我想我在观看和重温我想为你播放的最后一个场景时弄明白了，这是《Juiced》中最令人震惊的一个。它以O.J.解释即将发生的恶作剧开始，发生在二手车行。

