<v Tom Lange>不，你不应得那个。

00:40:04.400 --> 00:40:04.950
<v O.J. Simpson>I'ma get hurt.
<v O.J. Simpson>我会受伤的。

00:40:04.950 --> 00:40:11.030
<v Tom Lange>You do not deserve to get hurt. You do not deserve to get hurt. Don't do this.
<v Tom Lange>你不应得受伤。你不应得受伤。别这么做。

00:40:11.030 --> 00:40:14.190
<v O.J. Simpson>All I did was love Nicole That's all I did was love her.
<v O.J. Simpson>我所做的只是爱Nicole。我所做的只是爱她。

00:40:14.190 --> 00:40:14.720
<v Tom Lange>I understand.
<v Tom Lange>我理解。

00:40:14.720 --> 00:40:18.990
<v O.J. Simpson>I love everybody. I tried to show everybody my whole life that I love everybody.
<v O.J. Simpson>我爱每个人。我试图向每个人展示我的一生我都爱每个人。

00:40:18.990 --> 00:40:24.900
<v Tom Lange>We know that, and everybody loves you. Especially your family-- your mother, your kids.
<v Tom Lange>我们知道，每个人都爱你。特别是你的家人——你的母亲，你的孩子。

00:40:24.900 --> 00:40:28.980
<v O.J. Simpson>I'm just going to leave. I'm just going to go with Nicole. That's all I'ma do. That's all I'm trying to do.
<v O.J. Simpson>我只是要离开。我只是要和Nicole一起去。我所做的就是这个。我所做的就是这个。

00:40:28.980 --> 00:40:31.230
<v Tom Lange>Hey, listen. Don't give in now.
<v Tom Lange>嘿，听着。现在别放弃。

00:40:31.230 --> 00:40:34.770
<v O.J. Simpson>Ugh. I am so tired.
<v O.J. Simpson>呃。我太累了。

00:40:34.770 --> 00:40:35.790
<v Tom Lange>I know. I know.
<v Tom Lange>我知道。我知道。

00:40:35.790 --> 00:40:39.660
<v O.J. Simpson>I just want to be with Nicole.
<v O.J. Simpson>我只想和Nicole在一起。

00:40:39.660 --> 00:41:04.380
<v Nancy Updike>This had to have been one of most terrifying, inconsolable moments of O.J. Simpson's life, as he makes clear with every word, every groan. And watching Juiced , I kept looking for-- not even any specific emotion, but just any sign at all that this event had happened to him. That it affected him in some way. I didn't see one.
<v Nancy Updike>这一定是O.J. Simpson一生中最恐怖、最无法安慰的时刻之一，正如他用每一个字、每一次呻吟所清楚表明的那样。看《Juiced》时，我一直在寻找——甚至没有任何特定的情感，只是寻找他经历过这件事的任何迹象。它以某种方式影响了他。我没看到一个。

00:41:04.380 --> 00:41:05.580
<v O.J. Simpson>It was good for me.
<v O.J. Simpson>它对我很好。

00:41:05.580 --> 00:41:06.770
<v Man>Yeah?
<v Man>是吗？

00:41:06.770 --> 00:41:17.700
<v O.J. Simpson>Got me out of harm's way. [LAUGHS] A car that I personally made famous! [INAUDIBLE] was driving this thing. And if we wanted to get away, it was easy to get away.
<v O.J. Simpson>让我脱离了危险。[笑] 一辆我亲自让它出名的车！[听不清] 正在开这辆车。如果我们想离开，很容易离开。

00:41:17.700 --> 00:41:49.310
<v Nancy Updike>The way he's talking about the Bronco, there's no trace of what he experienced a decade before inside the Bronco, inside his own head. Instead, he seems to be remembering it, he seems to be seeing it, the same way we remember seeing it-- on TV. Looking at it from above on the highway, as it drove mile after mile. The emptiness in Juiced ? It's O.J. He's not there.
<v Nancy Updike>他谈论Bronco的方式，没有他十年前在Bronco里，在他自己脑子里经历过的事情的痕迹。相反，他似乎在回忆它，他似乎在看到它，就像我们记得看到它一样——在电视上。从高速公路上方看着它，随着它行驶了一英里又一英里。《Juiced》里的空虚？那是O.J.。他不在那儿。

00:41:49.310 --> 00:42:06.190
<v Ira Glass>Nancy Updike is one of the producers of our show. Coming up, a video that shows up too soon. And then, it is no longer too soon. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>Nancy Updike是我们节目的制片人之一。接下来，一段过早出现的视频。然后，它就不再过早了。那一分钟来自芝加哥公共广播电台，当我们的节目继续时。

00:42:06.190 --> 00:42:13.380
<v Ira Glass>This American Life from Ira Glass. Each week we've got a program, of course, which is a theme, bring you different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's show-- "too soon."
<v Ira Glass>来自Ira Glass的《美国生活》。每周我们都有一个节目，当然，这是一个主题，带给你关于这个主题的不同种类的故事。今天的节目——“太早了”。

00:42:13.380 --> 00:42:22.290
<v Zoe Chace>So my dad died about 10 years ago, very suddenly. And it was very terrible and shocking.
<v Zoe Chace>所以我父亲大约10年前去世了，非常突然。非常可怕和令人震惊。

00:42:22.290 --> 00:42:40.700
<v Ira Glass>We were talking about this week's program around the office, and one of our producers, Zoe Chace, remembered this story. It happened when she was right out of college, and she got the call. Her dad, out of the blue, had a heart attack and died. And her best friend, Rosa, came and got Zoe from Ohio, which is where she was living, and brought her back to New York City, where her family was.
<v Ira Glass>我们在办公室讨论这周的节目，我们的一位制片人Zoe Chace想起了这个故事。那发生在大学刚毕业时，她接到了那个电话。她的父亲，出人意料地心脏病发作去世了。她最好的朋友Rosa从俄亥俄州（她当时住的地方）接走了Zoe，把她带回了她的家人所在的纽约市。

00:42:40.700 --> 00:43:02.570
<v Zoe Chace>And it was awful. You know, it was just grim darkness. But then, somehow like the next day, Rosa was just pulling stuff out of the fridge to feed me with. And she pulls out the butter dish, you know? A glass butter dish. And it just shatters all over the floor.
<v Zoe Chace>那很糟糕。你知道，那只是阴沉的黑暗。但然后，不知何故就像第二天，Rosa只是从冰箱里拿出东西来喂我。她拿出了黄油盘，你知道吗？玻璃黄油盘。它在地板上打碎了。

00:43:02.570 --> 00:43:03.850
<v Ira Glass>You mean she drops it.
<v Ira Glass>你是说她摔了它。

00:43:03.850 --> 00:43:28.990
<v Zoe Chace>She drops it. And I just look at her, and I'm like, that was my dad's. (LAUGHING) It was just-- I was like, horrified! And my sister starts laughing, and I started laughing. Because it's not my dad's. It's just like a crappy butter dish from IKEA. And it was the first-- I think it was the first joke I remember. I remember it as the first joke of when my dad died.
<v Zoe Chace>她摔了它。我只是看着她，我心想，那是我的父亲的。（笑）我简直——我简直——我惊呆了！我妹妹开始笑，我也开始笑。因为它不是我父亲的。它只是宜家那种破黄油盘。那是第一——我想是我记得的关于父亲去世的第一件笑话。我把它记得是父亲去世后的第一个笑话。

00:43:28.990 --> 00:43:30.830
<v Ira Glass>How many days?
<v Ira Glass>多少天？

00:43:30.830 --> 00:43:34.220
<v Zoe Chace>It was like a day. It was like the day after.
<v Zoe Chace>就像一天。就像第二天。

00:43:34.220 --> 00:43:41.300
<v Ira Glass>So the whole idea of-- you know the old saying, comedy equals tragedy plus time, there was no time at all.
<v Ira Glass>所以那个关于——你知道那句老话，喜剧等于悲剧加时间，那时根本没有时间。

00:43:41.300 --> 00:43:45.140
<v Zoe Chace>Not at all. Yeah.
<v Zoe Chace>根本没有。是的。

00:43:45.140 --> 00:44:30.210
<v Ira Glass>There's a study where scientists actually tried to quantify exactly how much time has to pass after tragedy before comedy kicks in. And although nothing is more tedious than people sitting around theorizing about what is funny, it's interesting to see somebody get super brass tacks about this and try to actually define this thing that our whole program is about today. These researchers had over 1,000 people rate how funny some joke tweets about Hurricane Sandy were at different points in time. And before the hurricane hit land, people thought the tweets were funny. And then, unsurprisingly, while the hurricane was destroying homes, and knocking out power, and killing people, and in the immediate aftermath of all that, the tweets did not seem funny and in fact seemed kind of offensive.
<v Ira Glass>有一个研究，科学家们实际上试图量化悲剧发生后喜剧出现需要多少时间。虽然没有什么比人们坐在一起理论什么是有趣的更乏味的了，但看到有人对此变得超级务实并试图真正定义这个我们今天整个节目的主题很有趣。这些研究人员让超过1000人对一些关于飓风桑迪的有趣推文在不同时间点进行了评分。在飓风登陆之前，人们认为推文很有趣。然后，不出所料，当飓风摧毁房屋、切断电力并杀人时，以及在所有这些的直接后果中，推文看起来并不有趣，事实上看起来有点冒犯。

00:44:30.210 --> 00:45:02.780
<v Ira Glass>And then, it took-- they have a number-- it took 15 days. 15 days after the hurricane struck, the tweets started to seem funny again. Because the researchers-- Peter McGraw, Lawrence Williams, and Caleb Warren-- theorize for something like this to be funny, it has to seem threatening, but not too threatening. Once it stops being threatening, it stops being funny. And in fact, there comes a point after the storm struck-- it is 36 days after the storm struck-- that people start finding the tweets less and less funny.
<v Ira Glass>然后，它花了——他们有一个数字——它花了15天。飓风袭击后的15天，推文开始看起来又很有趣了。因为研究人员——Peter McGraw, Lawrence Williams, 和 Caleb Warren——理论认为要让这样的事情有趣，它必须看起来有威胁，但不要太有威胁。一旦它不再有威胁，它就不再有趣了。事实上，在飓风袭击后有一个点——是袭击后36天——人们开始发现推文越来越不有趣了。

00:45:02.780 --> 00:45:19.500
<v Ira Glass>And, to get back to Zoe, Zoe says, yeah, it was only a day after her dad's death that she joked about the butter dish. But the worst had already happened. The threat had past. If her dad had been alive and in imminent danger of dying, she said she couldn't have made the joke, wouldn't have had the impulse to.
<v Ira Glass>而且，回到Zoe，Zoe说，是的，在父亲去世后仅一天，她就开了黄油盘的玩笑。但最坏的情况已经发生了。威胁已经过去了。如果她的父亲还活着且处于死亡的迫在眉睫的危险中，她说她不可能开这个玩笑，不会有冲动。

00:45:19.500 --> 00:45:32.810
<v Zoe Chace>Because that just sounds so scary, that that would be all you were doing, is being afraid. But now, or right at that moment that I was in--
<v Zoe Chace>因为那听起来太可怕了，那将是你正在做的一切，就是感到害怕。但现在，或者就在我所处的那一刻——

00:45:32.810 --> 00:45:34.250
<v Ira Glass>Immediately after he died.
<v Ira Glass>在他去世后立即。

00:45:34.250 --> 00:45:43.310
<v Zoe Chace>Immediately after. I mean, nobody had been themselves, or said anything funny, or laughed about anything. It was super helpful.
<v Zoe Chace>去世后立即。我是说，没有人表现得像他们自己，或者说了任何有趣的事，或者嘲笑任何事情。这超级有帮助。

00:45:43.310 --> 00:45:45.360
<v Ira Glass>Helpful how?
<v Ira Glass>如何有帮助？

00:45:45.360 --> 00:45:59.980
<v Zoe Chace>Because I felt like myself, and it felt like my family. You know, we're not such a sincere group of people. And so to have that level of sincere emotion was pretty uncomfortable.
<v Zoe Chace>因为我感觉像我自己，这感觉像我的家人。你知道，我们不是那种真诚的群体。所以有那种程度的真诚情感非常不舒服。

00:45:59.980 --> 00:46:06.110
<v Ira Glass>[CHUCKLES] Wow. This has gotten so real.
<v Ira Glass>[笑] 哇。这变得太真实了。

00:46:06.110 --> 00:46:13.910
<v Zoe Chace>[CHUCKLES]
<v Zoe Chace>[笑]

00:46:13.910 --> 00:46:38.410
<v Ira Glass>Which brings us to this next story of a family, and something being too soon, and then knowing the right moment for the something to happen. We've arrived at Act 2 of our program. Act two, Pink Slip. When Amy Silverman was in her 20s, she had this friend who would go to LA sometimes and bring back these videos. And Amy and her friends would show the videos at parties. On VHS-- this is way back in the prehistoric days, before YouTube.
<v Ira Glass>这把我们带到了下一个关于家庭的故事，有些事情太早了，然后知道事情发生正确的时间。我们已经到达了节目的第二幕。第二幕，解雇通知。Amy Silverman在20多岁时，有这个朋友有时会去洛杉矶带回这些视频。Amy和她的朋友会在派对上放这些视频。在VHS上——那是很久以前的史前时代，在YouTube之前。

00:46:38.410 --> 00:46:45.440
<v Amy Silverman>This was the '90s. But in Phoenix. So it's probably like the '80s somewhere else.
<v Amy Silverman>那是90年代。但在凤凰城。所以它在别处可能是80年代。

00:46:45.440 --> 00:47:01.270
<v Ira Glass>And one of the films the guy would bring around to parties was called Pink Slip . Amy says she easily saw it a half dozen times, maybe it was more like a dozen. It always killed. Everybody liked it. It was an instructional video, and its lead character was this girl named Jill, who was just reaching puberty.
<v Ira Glass>那家伙带到派对上的电影之一叫《Pink Slip》（解雇通知）。Amy说她至少看了半打次，也许更像是一打。它总是很火。每个人都喜欢它。这是一个教学视频，主角是一个叫Jill的女孩，她刚刚进入青春期。

00:47:01.270 --> 00:47:17.070
<v Amy Silverman>I've never been able to figure out when exactly it was made. But probably like late '60s, early '70s. Kind of groovy living room, and they have very outdated hair. Very, very obviously staged. And that was what was so funny about it, really.
<v Amy Silverman>我从来没能弄清楚它到底是什么时候制作的。但大概像60年代末，70年代初。有点时髦的客厅，他们的发型非常过时。非常，非常明显是安排好的。那正是它如此有趣的原因，真的。

00:47:17.070 --> 00:47:27.630
<v Ira Glass>The film was about menstruation. And so if you're listening with a little kid right now, and you do not want to get into that subject with them right this second, take that into advisement. OK, here's a clip.
<v Ira Glass>电影是关于月经的。所以如果你现在和一个小孩子在听，而你不希望立刻和他们谈论那个话题，请注意这一点。好的，这是一个片段。

00:47:27.630 --> 00:47:30.160
<v Jill>Susie, do you have periods?
<v Jill>Susie，你有月经吗？

00:47:30.160 --> 00:47:42.220
<v Susie>Do I have periods? Jill, all women have periods about every four weeks for three or four days. When I'm on my period, blood from inside of my body comes outside from an opening between my legs.
<v Susie>我有月经吗？Jill，所有女性大约每四周有三到四天的月经。当我来月经时，我体内的血液从我双腿之间的一个开口流到外面。

00:47:42.220 --> 00:47:46.360
<v Jill>Well, Susie, what about my teacher? Does Miss Jones have periods?
<v Jill>嗯，Susie，那我的老师呢？Miss Jones有月经吗？

00:47:46.360 --> 00:47:54.410
<v Susie>Yes, Jill. Your teacher, Mrs. Jones, does have periods. All women have periods about every four weeks for three or four days.
<v Susie>是的，Jill。你的老师，Mrs. Jones，确实有月经。所有女性大约每四周有三到四天的月经。

00:47:54.410 --> 00:47:55.400
<v Dad>Hi, girls.
<v Dad>嗨，女孩们。

00:47:55.400 --> 00:47:56.260
<v Susie>Hi, Dad.
<v Susie>嗨，爸爸。

00:47:56.260 --> 00:47:58.510
<v Dad>What have you girls been talking about?
<v Dad>女孩们在谈论什么？

00:47:58.510 --> 00:48:03.940
<v Jill>About periods, Daddy! Dad, does Aunt Carol have periods?
<v Jill>关于月经，爸爸！爸爸，Carol姨妈有月经吗？

00:48:03.940 --> 00:48:07.480
<v Dad>Yes, Jill. All women have periods about every four weeks.
<v Dad>是的，Jill。所有女性大约每四周有月经。

00:48:07.480 --> 00:48:09.120
<v Ira Glass>So it's very repetitive.
<v Ira Glass>所以非常重复。

00:48:09.120 --> 00:48:16.770
<v Amy Silverman>And we would watch it and shriek with laughter. Not proud of it, but that is what happened. A lot.
<v Amy Silverman>我们会看它并尖叫着大笑。不以为傲，但这就是发生了什么。很多。

00:48:16.770 --> 00:48:23.330
<v Dad>Blood from inside a woman's body comes outside, from an opening between her legs, about every four weeks.
<v Dad>女性体内的血液从她双腿之间的一个开口流出来，大约每四周一次。

00:48:23.330 --> 00:48:25.620
<v Jill>But the blood? Won't it get on my clothes?
<v Jill>但血呢？它不会弄脏我的衣服吗？

00:48:25.620 --> 00:48:28.220
<v Dad>No, it won't. Because you use a sanitary pad.
<v Dad>不，不会。因为你使用卫生巾。

00:48:28.220 --> 00:48:29.880
<v Jill>A sanitary pad.
<v Jill>卫生巾。

00:48:29.880 --> 00:48:49.380
<v Ira Glass>Amy says it did register with her that there was something up with that girl, Jill. Something different. But she never really gave it much thought. And she and her friends would drink, and they'd watch this kitschy old film. And years later, when Amy was 37, she had her second child-- a daughter, Sophie. And Sophie had Down Syndrome.
<v Ira Glass>Amy说她确实意识到那个女孩，Jill，有点问题。有点不同。但她从来没怎么多想。她和她的朋友会喝酒，她们会看这部老土的电影。几年后，当Amy 37岁时，她有了第二个孩子——一个女儿，Sophie。Sophie患有唐氏综合症。

00:48:49.380 --> 00:49:23.350
<v Amy Silverman>So when Sophie was like two weeks old, all of a sudden I was driving down the street one day, and I went, oh my god, Pink Slip ! And suddenly, it all came together for me, and I realized that that video had been about a girl with Down Syndrome. And now I had a baby with Down Syndrome. And some day, I was going to have to figure out how to teach her about puberty. And then, as quickly as that thought came into my head, I shoved it out and replaced it with, I don't know, a need for diapers or something.
<v Amy Silverman>所以当Sophie两周大时，突然有一天我开车在街上，我心想，哦，天哪，《Pink Slip》！突然，一切都连接起来了，我意识到那个视频是关于一个患有唐氏综合症的女孩。而现在我有一个患有唐氏综合症的宝宝。总有一天，我得想办法教她关于青春期的事情。然后，当那个想法进入我的脑海时，我立刻把它挤了出去，换成了，我不知道，尿布的需求之类的。

00:49:23.350 --> 00:49:30.600
<v Ira Glass>Years pass, and by the time Sophie was 10, she had seen her older sister become an adolescent, and Sophie was obsessed.
<v Ira Glass>岁月流逝，当Sophie 10岁时，她看到她的大姐姐变成了青少年，Sophie痴迷了。

00:49:30.600 --> 00:49:34.940
<v Amy Silverman>She had a bra collection. She had a deodorant collection.
<v Amy Silverman>她有一个胸罩收藏。她有一个除臭剂收藏。

00:49:34.940 --> 00:49:42.260
<v Ira Glass>Not that she needed a bra or deodorant just yet, Amy says. There was one false alarm, where it seemed like she was growing hair in new places.
<v Ira Glass>并非她现在需要胸罩或除臭剂，Amy说。有一个误报，看起来她在新的地方长了头发。

00:49:42.260 --> 00:50:00.830
<v Amy Silverman>And she started jumping up and down. She ran down the hallway naked into her sister's room to show her. She grabbed my cell phone and called one of my friends, and she raced around the kitchen table as fast as she could, again and again, you know, just talking about her hair with my mom, with my friend, with my husband.
<v Amy Silverman>她开始蹦蹦跳跳。她赤身裸体跑过走廊进她姐姐的房间向她展示。她抓起我的手机打电话给我的朋友，她尽可能快地围着餐桌跑，一遍又一遍，你知道，只是和我的母亲，和我的朋友，和我的丈夫谈论她的头发。

00:50:00.830 --> 00:50:03.660
<v Ira Glass>Oh, she really wants to hit puberty.
<v Ira Glass>哦，她真的很想进入青春期。

00:50:03.660 --> 00:50:06.070
<v Amy Silverman>She really wants to hit puberty.
<v Amy Silverman>她真的很想进入青春期。

00:50:06.070 --> 00:50:22.030
<v Ira Glass>Amy and her husband decided to enroll Sophie in a class about puberty, a one-time seminar for kids with Down Syndrome, to answer their questions and teach them what they needed to know about what was going to happen to them, taught by this woman who is an expert on all that. And it was OK. It was fine. Though it didn't do the job for Sophie.
<v Ira Glass>Amy和她的丈夫决定给Sophie报一个关于青春期的课程，一个针对唐氏综合症儿童的一次性研讨会，回答他们的问题并教他们需要知道关于即将发生的事情，由这位在这一切方面都是专家的女人教授。这还可以。没问题。虽然它没对Sophie起作用。

00:50:22.030 --> 00:50:26.200
<v Amy Silverman>Sophie was-- you know, she was about to turn 12, and she was still asking questions.
<v Amy Silverman>Sophie是——你知道，她快12岁了，她还在问问题。

00:50:26.200 --> 00:50:28.190
<v Ira Glass>What were the questions she was asking?
<v Ira Glass>她在问什么问题？

00:50:28.190 --> 00:50:44.990
<v Amy Silverman>She wanted to know the basics about her period. About where the blood would come out, what she would use. Just basics. And I thought, you know what? Maybe I should show her Pink Slip.
<v Amy Silverman>她想知道关于月经的基础知识。关于血从哪里出来，她会用什么。只是基础知识。我想，你知道吗？也许我应该给她看《Pink Slip》。

00:50:44.990 --> 00:50:49.140
<v Ira Glass>This was honestly something she had never considered before. She'd never taken that video seriously.
<v Ira Glass>这诚实地是她以前从未考虑过的事情。她从来没认真对待那个视频。

00:50:49.140 --> 00:50:58.360
<v Amy Silverman>No. No! We made fun of it. So I got the video up for her, for her to see.
<v Amy Silverman>不。不！我们取笑它。所以我为她找到了视频，让她看。

00:50:58.360 --> 00:51:03.930
<v Ira Glass>Now, I know you recorded a conversation with her before you showed her the video. Let me just play that piece of tape. Here we go.
<v Ira Glass>现在，我知道你在给她看视频之前录下了和她的谈话。让我播放那段磁带。我们开始吧。

00:51:03.930 --> 00:51:06.540
<v Amy Silverman>Do you remember what we're talking about today?
<v Amy Silverman>你记得我们今天在谈论什么吗？

00:51:06.540 --> 00:51:08.540
<v Susie>Periods.
<v Susie>月经。

00:51:08.540 --> 00:51:12.150
<v Amy Silverman>Yep. And now, do you feel like you already know everything about them?
<v Amy Silverman>是的。现在，你觉得你已经知道关于它们的一切了吗？

00:51:12.150 --> 00:51:19.920
<v Sophie>Yup. Because last year, I had a puberty lesson. And they sang a song-- "Just Around the Corner."
<v Sophie>是的。因为去年，我上了一节青春期课。他们唱了一首歌——“就在拐角处”。

00:51:19.920 --> 00:51:30.120
<v Ira Glass>She's saying last year they did a puberty lesson, and they sang a song-- "Just Around the Corner." That's how she knows everything. Amy pulled up Pink Slip on her phone because, of course, it's on YouTube.
<v Ira Glass>她说去年他们上了一节青春期课，唱了一首歌——“就在拐角处”。这就是她知道的一切。Amy在她的手机上找到了《Pink Slip》，因为，当然，它在YouTube上。

00:51:30.120 --> 00:51:35.490
<v Amy Silverman>And, unlike me, Sophie loves to watch videos on YouTube. So she grabbed it and watched it.
<v Amy Silverman>而且，不像我，Sophie喜欢在YouTube上看视频。所以她抓起它看了。

00:51:35.490 --> 00:51:37.650
<v Susie>And so let's watch the video.
<v Susie>所以我们来看视频吧。

00:51:37.650 --> 00:51:38.530
<v Amy Silverman>So let's watch the video?
<v Amy Silverman>所以我们来看视频？

00:51:38.530 --> 00:51:38.880
<v Susie>Yeah.
<v Susie>是的。

00:51:38.880 --> 00:51:39.770
<v Amy Silverman>OK.
<v Amy Silverman>好的。

00:51:39.770 --> 00:51:41.700
<v Susie>Yes, what about periods?
<v Susie>是的，关于月经呢？

00:51:41.700 --> 00:51:44.060
<v Jill>Do you have periods?
<v Jill>你有月经吗？

00:51:44.060 --> 00:52:02.210
<v Ira Glass>In the recording that Amy made of Sophie watching Pink Slip, there is a moment where they both laugh. It's the most shocking moment in the film, and it really is kind of shocking. Again, if you are listening right now with a small child, know that I am about to say something very frank about menstruation. And just decide if you want to keep listening.
<v Ira Glass>在Amy录下的Sophie看《Pink Slip》的录音中，有一个时刻她们都笑了。这是电影中最令人震惊的时刻，它确实有点令人震惊。再说一次，如果你现在和一个小孩在听，知道我即将对月经说一些非常坦率的话。只需决定你是否想继续听。

00:52:02.210 --> 00:52:10.840
<v Ira Glass>The moment that Amy and Sophie laugh at in the video is one that Amy remembers very well from back when she used to watch the video with her friends at parties. It's kind of the big moment in the film.
<v Ira Glass>Amy和Sophie在视频中笑的那个时刻，是Amy记得很清楚的，回到她曾经和朋友在派对上看视频的时候。这确实是电影中的大时刻。

00:52:10.840 --> 00:52:28.550
<v Amy Silverman>Well, this was like the grabber at the parties. Where Jill would say to Susie, you know, I don't understand or something. And Susie would say, well, come into the bathroom with me, Jill. I have my period right now. And then sit down, and pull down her pants, and show her bloody pad.
<v Amy Silverman>嗯，这就像派对上的抓手。Jill会对Susie说，你知道，我不明白之类的。Susie会说，好吧，跟我进浴室，Jill。我正来月经呢。然后坐下，拉下裤子，展示她的带血护垫。

00:52:28.550 --> 00:52:30.280
<v Susie>This is a used sanitary pad.
<v Susie>这是一片用过的卫生巾。

00:52:30.280 --> 00:52:33.260
<v Jill>Look, there's blood on it.
<v Jill>看，上面有血。

00:52:33.260 --> 00:52:34.920
<v Susie>See?
<v Susie>看到了吗？

00:52:34.920 --> 00:52:38.540
<v Jill>You mean the blood's coming from inside your body now?
<v Jill>你是说血现在正从你的体内出来？

00:52:38.540 --> 00:52:38.710
<v Susie>That's right.
<v Susie>那是对的。

00:52:38.710 --> 00:52:39.080
<v Susie>[SNICKERS]
<v Susie>[偷笑]

00:52:39.080 --> 00:52:43.310
<v Susie>Blood is coming from inside my body through an opening between my legs.
<v Susie>血液正从我体内的开口通过我双腿之间流出来。

00:52:43.310 --> 00:52:45.900
<v Amy Silverman>For the record, I laughed first.
<v Amy Silverman>记录在案，我先笑的。

00:52:45.900 --> 00:52:46.570
<v Sophie>Yep.
<v Sophie>是的。

00:52:46.570 --> 00:52:57.980
<v Ira Glass>Just a minute later, after the sister shows the girl in the film how to throw away a used pad and start using a new pad, Sophie watches intently and quietly says--
<v Ira Glass>一分钟后，在电影中的姐姐向女孩展示了如何丢弃用过的护垫并开始使用新护垫后，Sophie专心地看着并安静地说——

00:52:57.980 --> 00:53:00.340
<v Sophie>I get it now.
<v Sophie>我明白了。

00:53:00.340 --> 00:53:11.300
<v Ira Glass>Back at the parties that Amy used to go to, when the scene drew the biggest laughs and the most comments, it was just too soon for Amy to get what the video would be good for-- for a girl like Sophie.
<v Ira Glass>回到Amy曾经去的那些派对，当那个场景引起最大的笑声和最多的评论时，Amy太早了，无法理解那个视频对什么有用——对像Sophie这样的女孩。

00:53:11.300 --> 00:53:16.870
<v Ira Glass>What was it like watching her watch this video that you knew so well?
<v Ira Glass>看她看你如此熟悉的视频是什么感觉？

00:53:16.870 --> 00:53:26.090
<v Amy Silverman>Completely surreal. Completely surreal. I mean, embarrassing.
<v Amy Silverman>完全超现实。完全超现实。我是说，尴尬。

00:53:26.090 --> 00:53:28.590
<v Ira Glass>Why was it embarrassing?
<v Ira Glass>为什么尴尬？

00:53:28.590 --> 00:53:32.010
<v Amy Silverman>Oh, I was embarrassed because I used to make fun of it.
<v Amy Silverman>哦，我很尴尬，因为我曾经取笑它。

00:53:32.010 --> 00:53:36.520
<v Ira Glass>But Sophie didn't know that.
<v Ira Glass>但Sophie不知道。

00:53:36.520 --> 00:53:44.070
<v Amy Silverman>No, but I don't feel like that really honored her. You know? My prior behavior.
<v Amy Silverman>不，但我感觉那真的没尊重她。你知道吗？我之前的行为。

00:53:44.070 --> 00:53:52.470
<v Ira Glass>Oh, I see what you're saying. Here you are. You've got this little girl who you love. And now, with her present, you're returning to the scene of the crime.
<v Ira Glass>哦，我明白你的意思了。你在这里。你有一个你爱的小女孩。而现在，在她面前，你正在回到犯罪现场。

00:53:52.470 --> 00:54:02.860
<v Amy Silverman>Yeah. I felt more ashamed than embarrassed. But I had to stop and think, huh, maybe the person who made that video really knew what they were doing.
<v Amy Silverman>是的。我感觉比尴尬更羞耻。但我不得不停下来思考，嗯，也许制作那个视频的人真的知道他们在做什么。

00:54:02.860 --> 00:54:07.700
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. That's the thing I was wondering, is did it give you a respect for the video?
<v Ira Glass>是的。那是我一直在想的事情，是它让你对视频产生了尊重吗？

00:54:07.700 --> 00:54:11.730
<v Amy Silverman>It did. It was really weird.
<v Amy Silverman>它确实。那真的很奇怪。

00:54:11.730 --> 00:54:16.400
<v Ira Glass>And so all the corny repetition in the whole thing, that's totally right for her.
<v Ira Glass>所以整个东西里所有的俗气重复，对她来说是完全正确的。

00:54:16.400 --> 00:54:23.180
<v Amy Silverman>That's what she needs. It just has to be slowed down. The learning has to be slowed down.
<v Amy Silverman>那正是她需要的。它只是需要减慢速度。学习必须减慢速度。

00:54:23.180 --> 00:54:29.390
<v Ira Glass>Do you feel like a certain amount of being a parent is realizing what an ass you were before you were a parent?
<v Ira Glass>你觉得成为父母的一部分是意识到在成为父母之前你是个混蛋吗？

00:54:29.390 --> 00:55:01.380
<v Amy Silverman>Yes. Absolutely. I mean, I was the one who-- I always tell people this. I was the one who would switch lines at Safeway if there was a person with a developmental disability bagging groceries. Because I just didn't want to deal. It would've taken, what? 15 seconds to nod and smile? And be a human being about it? Not me.
<v Amy Silverman>是的。绝对。我是说，我就是那个——我总是告诉人们这件事。我就是那个如果有一个残疾人在装杂货，我会切换队伍的人。因为我只是不想处理。那需要，什么？15秒来点头微笑？并为此做一个人类？不是我。

00:55:01.380 --> 00:55:19.680
<v Ira Glass>Amy's thought about showing Pink Slip to Sophie again. It's been a few months, and Sophie still has not gotten her period. Though Amy's not sure what Sophie would think of the video today. Because Sophie's able to tell that the girl in the video has Down Syndrome. And Sophie is going through a thing right now where she does not like to identify herself that way.
<v Ira Glass>Amy想再次给Sophie展示《Pink Slip》。已经几个月了，Sophie仍然没有来月经。虽然Amy不确定Sophie今天会对视频有什么看法。因为Sophie能看出视频里的女孩患有唐氏综合症。而Sophie现在经历着一件她不喜欢那样识别自己的事情。

00:55:19.680 --> 00:55:40.600
<v Amy Silverman>Not to get all serious on you, but Sophie is starting to realize that she's-- she's not going to ever have some of the things that her sister has. So recently, she asked me, am I going to have Down Syndrome when I grow up? And I said, you are. And she said, I don't want to. I don't want to have it.
<v Amy Silverman>不谈得太严肃，但Sophie开始意识到她——她永远不会拥有她姐姐拥有的一些东西。所以最近，她问我，我长大后会患有唐氏综合症吗？我说，你会的。她说，我不想要。我不想患有它。

00:55:40.600 --> 00:55:42.380
<v Ira Glass>Oh.
<v Ira Glass>哦。

00:55:42.380 --> 00:55:44.370
<v Amy Silverman>Yeah.
<v Amy Silverman>是的。

00:55:44.370 --> 00:55:49.900
<v Ira Glass>So what does her sister get that she doesn't get, that she sees right now?
<v Ira Glass>所以她姐姐拥有什么她没拥有，她现在看到的？

00:55:49.900 --> 00:56:19.910
<v Amy Silverman>She gets to go to a fancy art school that won't take Sophie. She gets to dance on point in ballet, which Sophie doesn't get to do because her feet aren't strong enough. She gets sleepover invitations Sophie doesn't get. And she gets that life in front of her. You know, she-- Sophie told me the other day that she doesn't want to have an aid at school anymore, because she wants to practice walking to class by herself for college.
<v Amy Silverman>她可以去一个Sophie不被录取的昂贵艺术学校。她可以在芭蕾舞中跳脚尖舞，Sophie不能做因为她的脚不够强壮。她得到Sophie得不到的过夜邀请。她有那条在她面前的生活。你知道，她——Sophie前几天告诉我，她不想再在学校有助手了，因为她想练习自己走去教室上大学。

00:56:19.910 --> 00:57:03.180
<v Ira Glass>What Sophie will get that her sister got, and she'll probably get it pretty soon, is her period. And Amy might show her Pink Slip again. Amy tried once to show the video to Sophie's older sister. And the older sister got to the part with the bloody pad and then tossed the phone back to Amy. But Sophie's different, and she liked the video. Pink Slip was what she needed.
<v Ira Glass>Sophie会得到什么她姐姐得到的，她可能会很快得到，就是月经。Amy可能会再次给她看《Pink Slip》。Amy曾经试过给Sophie的姐姐看视频。姐姐到了带血护垫的部分，然后把手机扔回给Amy。但Sophie不同，她喜欢那个视频。Pink Slip是她需要的。

